Understanding the Internet: A Global Network of Networks
Learning to use the internet begins with understanding what it actually is: 
a global system of interconnected computer networks. It is not a single entity but a vast infrastructure that allows devices to communicate using standard protocols. 
When you send an email or load a webpage, your data travels through multiple networks, routers, and servers across the world in milliseconds. 
Grasping this basic architecture helps users appreciate both the power and the complexity of the tool they use daily,
and it demystifies concepts like "the cloud," which is simply someone else's computer network.
